<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Head to Toes! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About My Body</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Head to Toes!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow two-step spoken instructions related to classroom activities. Interactive: Respond in a predictable pattern to simple questions about familiar things.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow two-step instructions related to body movements (e.g., "Raise your hand and wave." or "Touch your nose and clap your hands."). Can listen and respond to predictable patterns presented in rhymes, songs, and chants (e.g., "Put your left foot in, take your left foot out."). Can follow and answer questions about body parts using gestures or short answers (e.g., "Where is your hat?" "On my head.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Stage & Description</p></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</p></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Estimated Date and Time</p></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using familiar body parts. Teacher says, "Simon says touch your nose!" and students follow. This activates prior knowledge and gets students moving. Vocabulary: head, nose, hands, feet, mouth. Grammar: Imperative ("Touch your nose.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces new vocabulary (head, toes, hand, foot, arm, leg, knee, elbow, shoulder, finger) using colorful flashcards and pointing to their own body parts. For example, the teacher holds up a flashcard for "head" and says, "This is my head. Head!" while touching their head. The teacher models the pronunciation of /h/ in "hand" and "head" and /t/ in "toes" by exaggerating the sounds. Students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple questions and using gestures.
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 8px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to an elbow on a flashcard) "Is this a knee?" (Students respond: No!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher says) "Show me your finger!" (Students point to their fingers.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An engaging audio recording of a simple "Body Parts Action Song" (e.g., a simplified "Head, Shoulders, Knees, and Toes" or a similar TPR song). The song includes vocabulary: head, shoulders, knees, toes, eyes, ears, mouth, nose, hands, and actions like "touch," "bend."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying body parts and following directions presented in a song. Following two-step spoken instructions related to body movements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 8px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher plays the song. Students listen and point to the body parts on themselves or a large body poster as they hear them.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Do:</b> The teacher plays the song again. This time, students listen and perform the actions described in the song (e.g., "Touch your head," "Bend your knees").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> After the song, the teacher asks, "What is the song about? Is it about food or body parts?" (Students respond: Body parts!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Listening</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Following one- and two-step spoken instructions. Identifying body parts (head, toes, hand, foot, arm, leg, knee, elbow, shoulder, finger, eye, nose, mouth, ear). Grammar: Imperative + noun ("Touch your hand"), Present simple ("This is my leg."), Possessive adjective "my." Verbs: touch, raise, bend, point.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 8px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Listen and Touch Game"</b>
                            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher calls out a body part (e.g., "Touch your arm!") and students quickly touch that body part. The teacher repeats several times, varying the speed. This reinforces single-step instructions and vocabulary recognition.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Two-Step Body Commands"</b>
                            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher gives two-step instructions. Students must listen carefully and perform both actions. E.g., "Touch your head and clap your hands!" "Bend your knees and raise your arms!" "Point to your nose and wiggle your fingers!" The teacher demonstrates first and praises correct responses. This activity targets the learning outcome of following two-step instructions.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check: "Listen, Point, and Confirm"</b>
                            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. The teacher calls out a body part or a simple action (e.g., "Show me your shoulder," "Touch your toes!"). Students listen and point to the correct body part on their partner. The teacher walks around, listening and providing support. This encourages active listening and peer interaction. Teacher might ask: "Where is *your* knee?"</p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group game: "Musical Body Freeze Dance."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> The teacher plays upbeat music. Students dance freely. When the music stops, the teacher shouts a two-step instruction (e.g., "Freeze! Touch your elbow and lift your leg!"). Students must listen and freeze in the position. The teacher observes if students follow the instructions correctly. This combines listening with physical response in a dynamic way, focusing on fluency and comprehension of multi-step commands related to body parts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Directly uses body parts (head, toes, hand, foot, arm, leg, knee, elbow, shoulder) and actions (touch, lift, bend, stretch, point) in a fun, interactive way. Incorporates possessive adjectives "your" implicitly when giving instructions. Grammar: Imperative verbs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension, quick physical response to spoken two-step instructions, and collaborative interaction in a game setting.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Musical Body Freeze Dance." The teacher uses a simple checklist to note which students successfully follow one-step and two-step instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will listen to a final set of two-step instructions given by the teacher and perform the actions (e.g., "Raise your hand and point to your toes.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 8px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows one-step instructions correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows two-step instructions correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds with appropriate physical action.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will be given a small paper with two prompts:
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 8px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a happy face if you can hear and touch "my head" and "my toes."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a star if you can follow "Touch your nose and clap your hands."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>Student and Teacher Reflection</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher facilitates a brief class discussion.
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 8px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What body parts did you hear today?" (Students might call out "head," "hands," "knees," etc.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun to do with your body parts?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 8px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to engage students and improve their listening comprehension? (e.g., TPR activities, the song, clear modeling).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future lessons or for students who struggled with two-step instructions? (e.g., more visual cues, slower pace, simpler combinations).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students effectively followed the instructions and identified the target vocabulary?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job listening to all the body parts and following instructions today! Next time, we will *read* a fun story about our body parts and find some of the words we learned today!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>